[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Security Testing and OAuth 2.0 / API Key Management in Postman"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Securing modern APIs requires rigorous testing and proper handling of credentials. This tutorial covers how to configure API keys, implement OAuth 2.0 authorization flows, and perform basic security testing using Postman."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Managing API Keys Securely"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Hardcoding API keys in your requests is a major security risk. Postman Environments allow you to store sensitive keys securely using environment variables."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Create a new Environment in Postman and name it 'Production Security'."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Add a variable named 'api_key' and set its type to 'secret'."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reference the variable in your request headers as {{api_key}} instead of exposing the raw string."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Configuring OAuth 2.0 Authorization"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"OAuth 2.0 is the industry standard for token-based authentication. Postman simplifies acquiring and managing access tokens via the Authorization tab."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Select your request, go to the 'Authorization' tab, and choose 'OAuth 2.0' from the Type dropdown."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Set Grant Type to 'Authorization Code' or 'Client Credentials' depending on your architecture."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Fill in the Auth URL, Access Token URL, Client ID, and Client Secret."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Click 'Get New Access Token' to trigger the OAuth handshake and save the token to your environment."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Automated Security Testing in Postman"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can write automated tests in the 'Scripts' > 'Post-response' (Tests) tab to verify that your security controls are actively working."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"pm.test("Status code is 401 Unauthorized for missing token", function () {
    pm.response.to.have.status(401);
});

pm.test("Response does not expose sensitive headers", function () {
    pm.response.to.not.have.header("X-Powered-By");
});"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Never commit environment files containing real secrets to public version control systems like GitHub."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Regularly rotate your API keys and OAuth client secrets."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Test negative scenarios, such as expired tokens and tampered payload signatures, to ensure robust API resilience."}]
